A1. Something flappers do with their eyes. ~ FLIRT
A5. Face. ~ MAP
A7. An Eastside lady. ~ SKIRT
A11. What people usually open when they go in a cold house. ~ DOOR
A13. This is full of hops. ~ TOAD
A14. Competent. ~ ABLE
A16. Synonym for a word meaning "after." ~ AFTER
A19. What a blue shirt gets in a Chinese laundry. ~ TORN
A21. Something butchers do when you don't pay your bill. ~ SUE
A22. This is nothing. ~ NIL
A23. Amalgamated Association of Americans (init.). ~ AAA
A24. Instead of getting peppery, this man's wife got salty. ~ LOT
A25. An order that Klansmen would like to execute (abbr.). ~ KC
A27. A flower. (He loves me, he loves me not.) ~ DAISY
A30. Latin for against (abbr.). ~ VS
A31. This is putting two and two together. ~ KISS
A34. People go all to pieces over this (abbr.). ~ TNT
A35. A dog kennel. ~ SHOE
A38. Something artists do. (Careful now!) ~ DRAW
A40. Where Fifth avenue bus rides are most enjoyed. ~ ATOP
A42. Reverential fear. ~ AWE
A43. Place where a lot of bad actors can be found. ~ THEATRE
A44. A buttress. ~ EWE
A45. Where there's a will there's this. (Not lawyer.) ~ AWAY
A47. This is one way to get bread and butter. ~ EARN
A49. Another way of spelling the third K. ~ CLAN
A50. What girls mean when they say no. ~ YES
A52. Kleptomaniacs don't live in a fog but everything they touch is this. ~ MIST
A54. A finishing touch (abbr.). ~ KO
A55. These kind of stockings are "de trop." ~ LISLE
A57. Part of the verb "be." ~ IS
A59. A modern highway robbery. ~ INN
A61. Something waiters wait for. ~ TIP
A62. Hard water. ~ ICE
A63. The exploited result of monkey glands. ~ PEP
A64. Lions. ~ LEOS
A66. These are often chewing the fat. ~ TEETH
A68. Something girls do in automobiles. ~ RIDE
A69. A well-known canal. ~ ERIE
A71. Usual beginning of bedtime stories. ~ ONCE
A73. What a man does shortly after he buys a second-hand car. ~ SELLS
A74. What the U. S. A. is. (Now you tell one!) ~ DRY
A75. A wet spot in a dry country. ~ OASIS

D1. A kick in the pants. ~ FLASK
D2. Well known kind of rumor and roomer. ~ IDLE
D3. Puzzle hounds know this means fish eggs. ~ ROE
D4. A preposition. ~ TO
D6. Something few actors do. ~ ACT
D7. This is so. ~ SO
D8. A wery krazy enimal. ~ KAT
D9. This is often kept on a pedestal. ~ IDOL
D10. Sheik's homes. ~ TENTS
D12. A prohibition officer's party. ~ RAID
D13. Something waiters carry. ~ TRAY
D15. An iron man. ~ BUCK
D17. This is the way a gold-digger leaves you, and I don't mean maybe. ~ FLAT
D18. Men may not be men in this part of the country but neither are women governors. ~ EAST
D20. What rovers do in June. ~ ROVE
D26. United States Reserves (abbr.). ~ USR
D28. These people are crazy about insane asylums. ~ INMATES
D29. Pronoun. (That's a big help.) ~ WHO
D32. The kind of a girl that's hard to find. ~ IDEAL
D33. This fellow doesn't have any worries about coal. ~ SATAN
D35. Walter Johnson has a lot of this. ~ STEAM
D36. What a freshly painted door does when you push it. ~ OPENS
D37. There's some in every family tree. ~ SAP
D39. Dora uses this interrogative often. ~ WHY
D40. Part of the verb "be." (That's another big help.) ~ ARE
D41. An egg plant. (The first letter is H and the last is N.) ~ HEN
D46. There is a great white one, a broad one, and a milky one. ~ WAY
D48. First name of a famous sleeper. ~ RIP
D49. An ice cream receptacle. ~ CONE
D50. What dogs do when their tails are stepped on. ~ YIPE
D51. It's rather hard to see through this. ~ SLIT
D53. What a football score is when the score is 0—0. ~ TIED
D54. Slays. ~ KILLS
D55. The light of modern spellers. ~ LITE
D56. The only thing that can get in the last word with a woman. ~ ECHO
D58. These are supported by a bridge (abbr.). ~ SPECS
D60. Christmas in France. ~ NOEL
D63. Necessities in a custard pie comedy. ~ PIES
D65. Stewed Rhinoceros Legs (init.) ~ SRL
D67. Borro wed by Brutus. ~ EAR
D68. Initials of a well-known radio corporation. ~ RCA
D70. Same as 57 horizontal. ~ IS
D72. What wives mean when they say no. ~ NO


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
A66: These are often chewing the fat. → printed clue number is 65 (should be 66)
D67: Borro wed by Brutus. → Borrowed

